Once we define the DGAlgebraMap, it is a good idea to check to see if it indeed
satisfies the Leibniz rule.  This can be checked by using isWellDefined.

+--------------------+
|i5 : isWellDefined f|
|                    |
|o5 = false          |
+--------------------+

Oops!  Let's try that again.

+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : g = dgAlgebraMap(K1,K2,matrix{{Y_(1,2),Y_(1,3)}})        |
|                                                              |
|o6 = map (R[Y   ..Y   ], R[T   ..T   ], {Y   , Y   , a, b, c})|
|             1,1   1,3      1,1   1,2     1,2   1,3           |
|                                                              |
|o6 : DGAlgebraMap                                             |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : isWellDefined g                                          |
|                                                              |
|o7 = true                                                     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+

One can lift a ring homomorphism in degree zero to a map of DGAlgebras (up to a
specified degree) using liftToDGMap.  This is helpful in some of the internal
functions of the DGAlgebras package, such as computing the map induced on Tor
algebras by a RingMap.

