Operations on DG Ideals -- Sum, product, power, intersection, colon, and other ideal-algebra operations on DG ideals
********************************************************************************************************************

A DG ideal of a DG algebra A is a graded ideal of A.natural that is closed
under the differential d of A.  The *note DGIdeal: DGIdeal, type wraps such an
ideal and exposes the standard M2 *note Ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal, operations,
each of which preserves d-closure and returns a new *note DGIdeal: DGIdeal,.

Constructor: *note dgIdeal: dgIdeal, takes a List, Matrix, or Ideal of elements
of A.natural and iteratively d-saturates the ideal they generate until it is
closed under d.  Warning: if an input element is not a cycle, the d-saturation
enlarges the ideal (possibly to the unit ideal).

+---------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[x, y]                              |
|                                                   |
|o1 = R                                             |
|                                                   |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                                |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : A = koszulComplexDGA R                        |
|                                                   |
|o2 = {Ring => R                          }         |
|      Underlying algebra => R[T   ..T   ]          |
|                               1,1   1,2           |
|      Differential => {x, y}                       |
|                                                   |
|o2 : DGAlgebra                                     |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : Anat = A.natural                              |
|                                                   |
|o3 = Anat                                          |
|                                                   |
|o3 : PolynomialRing, 2 skew commutative variable(s)|
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : I = dgIdeal(A, {x_Anat})                      |
|                                                   |
|o4 = DGIdeal of Anat                               |
|     generators => | x |                           |
|                                                   |
|o4 : DGIdeal                                       |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : J = dgIdeal(A, {y_Anat})                      |
|                                                   |
|o5 = DGIdeal of Anat                               |
|     generators => | y |                           |
|                                                   |
|o5 : DGIdeal                                       |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : isWellDefined I                               |
|                                                   |
|o6 = true                                          |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : isDGIdeal(A, I.natural)                       |
|                                                   |
|o7 = true                                          |
+---------------------------------------------------+

Ideal-algebra operations all preserve d-closure by elementary arguments (sum:
d(I+J) \subset dI + dJ \subset I + J; product: by the Leibniz rule;
intersection: direct; power: induction; colon: Leibniz + f J \subset I implies
(df) J \subset I + f(dJ) \subset I). Concretely, they all return well-defined
DGIdeals.

+-----------------------------------+
|i8 : isWellDefined(I + J)          |
|                                   |
|o8 = true                          |
+-----------------------------------+
|i9 : isWellDefined(I * J)          |
|                                   |
|o9 = true                          |
+-----------------------------------+
|i10 : isWellDefined(I^3)           |
|                                   |
|o10 = true                         |
+-----------------------------------+
|i11 : isWellDefined intersect(I, J)|
|                                   |
|o11 = true                         |
+-----------------------------------+
|i12 : isWellDefined(I : J)         |
|                                   |
|o12 = true                         |
+-----------------------------------+

Equality, containment, and reduction mirror the usual behavior on *note Ideal:
(Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,.

+--------------------------------+
|i13 : isSubset(I, I + J)        |
|                                |
|o13 = true                      |
+--------------------------------+
|i14 : I != I + J                |
|                                |
|o14 = true                      |
+--------------------------------+
|i15 : (x_Anat^2 % I) == 0       |
|                                |
|o15 = true                      |
+--------------------------------+
|i16 : (y_Anat^2 % I) == y_Anat^2|
|                                |
|o16 = true                      |
+--------------------------------+

Quotient DG algebras: A / I builds the DG algebra whose underlying ring is
A.natural / I.natural, with differential descending from d (well-defined
precisely because I is d-closed).

+----------------------------------+
|i17 : B = A / I                   |
|                                  |
|o17 = {Ring => R                 }|
|                             Anat |
|       Underlying algebra => ---- |
|                               x  |
|       Differential => {0, y}     |
|                                  |
|o17 : DGAlgebra                   |
+----------------------------------+
|i18 : class B                     |
|                                  |
|o18 = DGAlgebra                   |
|                                  |
|o18 : Type                        |
+----------------------------------+
|i19 : isWellDefined B             |
|                                  |
|o19 = true                        |
+----------------------------------+

Compatibility: any operation that takes an *note Ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,
and lives on I.natural can be reached via I.natural, but the wrapped operations
on DGIdeal return DGIdeals (so d-closure is certified on the result by running
through *note dgIdeal: dgIdeal, once more).

See also
========

  * *note DGIdeal: DGIdeal, -- The class of d-closed graded ideals of a DG
    algebra
  * *note dgIdeal: dgIdeal, -- Construct a DG ideal from a list, matrix, or
    ideal of generators
  * *note DGIdeal ** Ring: DGIdeal _st_st Ring, -- Base change of a DG ideal
    along a ring map
* Menu:

* DGIdeal::                     The class of d-closed graded ideals of a DG algebra
* dgIdeal::                     Construct a DG ideal from a list, matrix, or ideal of generators
* isDGIdeal::                   Test whether an ideal of A.natural is closed under the differential of A
* isWellDefined(DGIdeal): isWellDefined_lpDGIdeal_rp.  Verify that a DGIdeal has correct structure and is d-closed
* DGIdeal + DGIdeal::           Sum of two DG ideals
* DGIdeal * DGIdeal: DGIdeal _st DGIdeal.  Product of two DG ideals
* DGIdeal ^ ZZ::                Nonnegative integer power of a DG ideal
* intersect(DGIdeal,DGIdeal): intersect_lpDGIdeal_cmDGIdeal_rp.  Intersection of two DG ideals
* DGIdeal _colon_ DGIdeal: DGIdeal _co DGIdeal.  Ideal quotient (colon) of two DG ideals
* DGIdeal == DGIdeal::          Equality and containment of DG ideals
* RingElement % DGIdeal::       Reduce a ring element modulo a DG ideal
* mingens(DGIdeal): mingens_lpDGIdeal_rp.  Basic accessors on a DG ideal
* prune(DGIdeal): prune_lpDGIdeal_rp.  Trim a DG ideal down to a minimal generating set
* DGAlgebra / DGIdeal::         Quotient DG algebra by a DG ideal
* isHomogeneous(DGIdeal): isHomogeneous_lpDGIdeal_rp.  Predicates on a DG ideal
