dgAlgebraMultMap -- Returns the chain map corresponding to multiplication by a cycle.
*************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        phi = dgAlgebraMultMap(A,z)
  * Inputs:
      * A, an instance of the type *note DGAlgebra: DGAlgebra,, 
      * z, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, 
  * Outputs:
      * phi, a *note map of complexes: (Complexes)ComplexMap,, 

Description
===========

If A is a DGAlgebra, and z is a cycle of A, then left multiplication of A by z
gives a chain map from A to A.  This command converts A to a complex using
*note toComplex: toComplex,, and constructs a *note ComplexMap:
(Complexes)ComplexMap, that represents left multiplication by z. This command
is used to determine the module structure that is computed in *note
homologyModule: homologyModule,.

+------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y,z]/ideal{x^3,y^3,z^3}     |
|                                          |
|o1 = R                                    |
|                                          |
|o1 : QuotientRing                         |
+------------------------------------------+
|i2 : KR = koszulComplexDGA R              |
|                                          |
|o2 = {Ring => R                          }|
|      Underlying algebra => R[T   ..T   ] |
|                               1,1   1,3  |
|      Differential => {x, y, z}           |
|                                          |
|o2 : DGAlgebra                            |
+------------------------------------------+
|i3 : z1 = x^2*T_(1,1)                     |
|                                          |
|      2                                   |
|o3 = x T                                  |
|        1,1                               |
|                                          |
|o3 : R[T   ..T   ]                        |
|        1,1   1,3                         |
+------------------------------------------+
|i4 : phi = dgAlgebraMultMap(KR,z1)        |
|                                          |
|          3                  1            |
|o4 = 1 : R  <-------------- R  : 0        |
|               {1} | x2 |                 |
|               {1} | 0  |                 |
|               {1} | 0  |                 |
|                                          |
|          3                       3       |
|     2 : R  <------------------- R  : 1   |
|               {2} | 0 x2 0  |            |
|               {2} | 0 0  x2 |            |
|               {2} | 0 0  0  |            |
|                                          |
|          1                      3        |
|     3 : R  <------------------ R  : 2    |
|               {3} | 0 0 x2 |             |
|                                          |
|o4 : ComplexMap                           |
+------------------------------------------+

As you can see, the degree of phi is the homological degree of z:

+---------------------------------+
|i5 : degree phi == first degree z|
|                                 |
|o5 = true                        |
+---------------------------------+

Care is also taken to ensure the resulting map is homogeneous if R and z are:

+----------------------+
|i6 : isHomogeneous phi|
|                      |
|o6 = true             |
+----------------------+

One may then view the action of multiplication by the homology class of z upon
taking the induced map in homology:

+-------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : Hphi = prune HH(phi); (Hphi_0,Hphi_1,Hphi_2)|
|                                                 |
|o8 = (0, {6} | 0 1 0 |, {9} | 0 0 1 |)           |
|         {6} | 0 0 1 |                           |
|         {6} | 0 0 0 |                           |
|                                                 |
|o8 : Sequence                                    |
+-------------------------------------------------+

